              "text": "(1) For most of us, a normal day starts with one sunrise. For the astronauts on the International Space Station, there are about sixteen. The station travels around the Earth roughly every ninety minutes, about 400 kilometres above our heads, so day and night follow each other very quickly.\n\n(2) Living without gravity changes almost everything. Water does not pour; it floats in drops. Astronauts sleep in small sleeping bags attached to the wall so that they do not drift around while they are asleep. Even eating is a challenge: crumbs can float into equipment, so bread is often replaced by flat tortillas.\n\n(3) The biggest danger, however, is invisible. In weightlessness, muscles and bones do not have to work against gravity, and they slowly become weaker. To fight this, astronauts exercise for around two hours every day on special machines, including a running machine to which they are held by straps.\n\n(4) Water is another problem. Sending water into space is very expensive, so the station recycles almost everything, including sweat and even urine. After cleaning, the water is often purer than the tap water many people drink on Earth.\n\n(5) Scientists believe that the station is a laboratory for the future. Experiments there study how plants grow, how the human body changes and how materials behave without gravity. This knowledge will be essential if humans one day travel to Mars, a journey that could take many months each way.\n\n(6) Is life in space worth all the difficulty? Most astronauts say yes. Many describe the moment they first saw the whole Earth from above as life-changing. Seeing how thin the atmosphere looks from space, they say, makes them want to protect the planet.",
